[h1]Radio Survivor[/h1]

[b]Build 42.21 · Singleplayer & Multiplayer · TEST VERSION[/b]

Knox County, July 1993. You are not alone any more: voices answer on the radio, and groups of survivors live, fight and fortify in the county. The radio characters and the NPCs are played by an AI through a small program you run yourself, [b]the bridge[/b] (see below). Without it the game stays fully playable; the voices and the NPCs just stay silent.

[b]This is a test version.[/b] Expect bugs and balance changes. Start a new game, keep a backup of any save you care about, and report problems in the comments with your [i]console.txt[/i] and the bridge output.

[h2]Three mods in this item: what to tick[/h2]

Subscribe once, then tick in the Mods list what you want. Radio Survivor and Living Knox each work without the other.

[list]
[*][b]Radio Survivor[/b] (batman_RadioSurvivor): the radio, six AI voices, the Radio tab of the notebook, factions.
[*][b]Living Knox[/b] (batman_LivingKnox): groups of NPC survivors with homes, needs, routines and fights.
[*][b]Radio Survivor Core[/b] (batman_RadioSurvivorCore): what both share: missions and their journal, the link with the bridge, character identity, conversation history, reputation, the notebook. The game enables it for you; on its own it adds nothing playable.
[/list]

[b]Radio Survivor alone:[/b] voices, radio, notebook. [b]Living Knox alone:[/b] you talk to an NPC in the normal chat (/say) near them, or with the right-click menu [b]Talk[/b]; it answers in a speech bubble, and its missions go to the notebook. [b]Both:[/b] you talk to NPCs through the notebook (Ctrl+G) and Radio Survivor adds relations and agreements with the groups.

[b]Known limit:[/b] without Radio Survivor, delivering and claiming the reward of an NPC mission is not possible yet, and NPCs do not speak first. The bridge is one program whatever you tick.

[h2]What it adds[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Voices on the radio:[/b] Walt, a nurse, a deserter, a besieged family, a pirate station and a smuggler, each on a real frequency drawn for each game. Find their frequency on notes in the loot, then talk with any transmitting radio held in hand or worn on the back ([b]Transmit[/b] section of the radio window). They remember you, give advice, news and missions.
[*][b]Field notebook[/b] ([b]Notebook[/b] button): contacts, radio history by frequency, missions, rewards and your reputation.
[*][b]Living Knox survivor groups:[/b] 3 to 5 groups of human NPCs with a home, a leader, real supplies, needs and routines (guard, sleep, meals, scavenging, garden). They defend and fortify their home, fight zombies, and keep living off screen. Settlers, neutrals and raiders: raiders may warn, raid your base or take revenge.
[*][b]Missions:[/b] deliver, scout, find a missing person, clear a place, recover a stash, signal. The server checks every objective; come back and report.
[*][b]Rescue, smuggler, chronicle:[/b] a besieged family calls for help; the Ohio smuggler trades over the radio; the pirate station reads the server news and the names of the dead.
[*][b]Last pages:[/b] when a character dies, a handwritten journal of their life is left on the body.
[*][b]Reputation:[/b] bad, neutral or good, earned through missions and kept by the server.
[/list]

The server decides everything: the AI only writes words and picks from closed lists. It never creates items, positions or rules.

[h2]Required mods[/h2]

[list]
[*][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3811010882]Signal Smoke[/url] (batman_SignalSmoke): all three mods.
[*][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3817018338]Belt Walkie-Talkie[/url] (batman_BeltRadio), which itself requires [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3694097672]TooltipLib[/url]: Radio Survivor only.
[/list]
Load order: TooltipLib, Belt Walkie-Talkie, Signal Smoke, Radio Survivor Core, Radio Survivor, Living Knox.

[b]Not compatible[/b] (Living Knox) with Bandits! and Project A-Life: one NPC engine per game.

[h2]The bridge: install and start[/h2]

Project Zomboid's Lua has no network access, so the game and the bridge exchange text files in a shared folder, and the bridge calls the AI. A Workshop mod cannot start a program: [b]run the bridge yourself, on the machine that runs the server[/b] (your PC in singleplayer). Players who join a server install nothing but the mods.

[b]You need:[/b] Python 3.10 or newer (no package to install), the five bridge files kept together (radio_bridge.py, npc_protocol.py, persona_store.py, bulletin.py, last_pages.py; during the test phase they are handed out with the test build), and an API key from a provider compatible with the OpenAI API (/chat/completions).

[b]1. Key.[/b] Copy [i].env.example[/i] to [i].env[/i] next to radio_bridge.py and fill in OPENAI_KEY, OPENAI_BASE_URL (for example https://api.openai.com/v1) and OPENAI_MODEL. Never share this file.

[b]2. Check.[/b] [i]python radio_bridge.py --test "Hello?"[/i] prints a few lines from Walt: key, address and model are right. Add [i]--offline[/i] to check without a key.

[b]3. Start.[/b] [i]python radio_bridge.py[/i] and leave the window open while you play. Wait for [i]Bridge ready[/i]. The start order (game or bridge first) does not matter.

[list]
[*][b]Singleplayer and game hosted from the menu:[/b] nothing else; the default exchange folder is %USERPROFILE%\Zomboid\Lua\batman_RadioSurvivorCore.
[*][b]Dedicated server (Windows or Linux):[/b] run the bridge under the server account. With -cachedir=X, add [i]--exchange X/Lua/batman_RadioSurvivorCore[/i]. Start it with the server (Task Scheduler, systemd service, or a second container sharing the server volume). While the bridge runs, that folder holds bridge-alive.txt: if not, it is the wrong folder.
[*][b]One bridge per folder:[/b] a second one refuses to start (double answers, double cost).
[*][i]--offline[/i]: no AI, no cost, the voices echo you (to test the setup). [i]--debug[/i]: full log (contains the dialogues, do not share as is).
[/list]

[b]Cost:[/b] every message is one AI call. The sandbox option [b]MaxPerHour[/b] (60 by default, all players together) caps the radio; never set it to 0 (unlimited) on a public server. Set a monthly spending limit at your provider too. Usage is logged in token_usage.jsonl in the exchange folder.

[b]Without the bridge:[/b] the radio answers "Only static...", the evening bulletin and the last pages fall back to short fixed texts, and NPCs stay silent. Everything else keeps working.

[h2]Sandbox options[/h2]

One page per mod: [b]Radio Survivor[/b] (message cap and delay, spontaneous calls and their language, frequency notes in the loot, bulletin hour, last pages, NPC initiatives, rescue delay) and [b]Living Knox[/b] (number and kind of groups, group size, raids, revenge delay, combat distances).

